  "preamble": "In Navajo, Proto-Athabaskan stem-final *tɬʼ became the voiced lateral l (written l, after a long high-tone vowel in many stems), whereas most other Athabaskan languages have the fricative ɬ or a plain affricate. The change goes with the loss of stem-final glottalization that fed Navajo tone (Kingston 2005).",
